A||||21||sluppy123||New Engine-Hard to start when hot||||9
Z||000000||sluppy123||09-15-1999||08:54 PM||||I Recently replaced my worn out v-6 engine in my 87' GT. The car startes fine when it's been sitting over night or it has cooled off completly. But If I shut it off and try to start it again it Cranks for about 5-7 seconds before it will fire. The engine only has 200 miles on it, All of the Ingition is new Including a MSD Blaster coil, And yes the timing is set acording to the Haynes Manual and the VECI label under the deck lid. <P>Any ideas???||||reg||9
Z||000001||mwbackus||09-15-1999||09:48 PM||||Leaking fuel injector(s).<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||09-15-1999||10:33 PM||||A friends fuel injected '83 z-28 did something like this and it was an ecm component failure. The ecm would not trigger the fuel pump when first turning on the switch, only when the engine built oil pressure and tripped the low oil pressure switch would the pump run. Cranked fine when cold because the oil was thick and had plenty of oil pressure. When hot, it took a little longer because the hot oil took longer to build enough pressure for the switch. Replaced the ecm and it has been fine every since.||||reg||1
Z||000003||SloPonti||09-15-1999||10:55 PM||||this could also be a fuel pump problem or the fuel pump wiring or relay!  a simple test is to turn the ignition on part way so that the acceseries turn on and you should here the fuel pump turn on for two seconds then shut off since you are not starting the car!  but it the fuel pump checks out then it could also be the ecm!  my ecm took a crap but my car would not start at all!  but just remember the ecm is not cheap so do some tests first to make sure that the problem is not something else!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||mwbackus||09-16-1999||05:17 AM||||     I see this same problem occuring on so many of the Fiero V6's that I sometimes forget to explain why it has this effect...  <BR>      When the motor is cold, it will indeed start VERY well, since the mixture will be even further enrichened from the leaking injector.  When the motor is at or near operating temp and you shut it off, the fuel rail is still pressurized.  As the car sets for a few minutes, fuel will leak from the bad injector causing a very rich or "flooded" condition upon the first few cranks of a "hot" startup. (hence the hard starting).  This "flooded" condition can usually be verified by a small amount of carbon (ie..black smoke) emitted from the exhaust upon start up.  <BR>     If the motor is restarted almost immediatly, or if sufficient time has transpired for the fuel rail to equalize and the leaked fuel to evaporate, it should start nearly as well as it did on the first start of the day.<P>     Hopefully, from the description I have given above, it should not be all that hard to determine if this is the cause of your problem.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000005||sluppy123||09-16-1999||06:21 PM||||Anybody have any other suggestion's?<P>I removed the fuel rail and found that 3 of the injectors were leaking. I replaced them and the problem is still there. I checked to see if the fuel pump turns on when the key is turned on and it does!! <P>Any suggestions????<p>[This message has been edited by sluppy123 (edited 09-19-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Speadraser||09-20-1999||10:48 AM||||I had a similar problem on mine.  Try replacing the coolant temperature sensor.  It is located on the intake manifold near the water pump.  Let us know how it turns out.<BR>Speady||||reg||14
Z||000007||mwbackus||09-20-1999||12:36 PM||||Thats quite interesting.  What method did you use to determine which of the injectors were leaking?  Perhaps you missed one?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||sluppy123||09-20-1999||10:26 PM||||MWBACKUS-to determine which fuel injectors were bad I pulled the fuel rail out and pressurized it, I could see the fuel dripping out of them. <P>SPEADY- I replaced the coolant temp sensor, but there was no improvement<P>Any other sugestions??????????????????????||||reg||1
Z||000009||mwbackus||09-20-1999||10:59 PM||||The symtoms indicate that fuel is somehow reaching the intake while the motor is shut off.  If you have determined that the injectors and cold start system are both operating correctly, then the ECM would be the next logical area of suspect.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fab4mula||09-21-1999||02:59 PM||||In the case of my Formula (EXACTLY the same condition), it appears to be the injectors.  They flood the engine, and when it finally kicks over, I get white (not blue) smoke, and it then runs fine.  Runs rich, lousy gas mileage, but it runs fine.||||reg||3
Z||000011||Patrick||09-21-1999||03:51 PM||||<BR>Fab4mula, I've reprinted this from an earlier post of mine in another thread.<P>Blue smoke - oil<BR>White smoke - water<BR>Black smoke - rich mixture<BR>Black smoke with flames - your Fiero is on fire (sorry)<P>Your <B>white</B> smoke is probably condensation in the exhaust system simply evaporating (if your coolant level doesn't appear to be dropping). I doubt that it's linked to any leaking injector problem.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Speadraser||09-21-1999||04:18 PM||||Check for a high TPS voltage with throttle closed(should be between .2V and 1.25V) If it's over 2.5V check the blk wire it should read 0 ohms resistance to ground.  Make shure your fuel pressure is high enough and steady at initial start up. I believe its 42Lbs/in2 +/- 2.  Have you tried filling up with a higher grade of gasoline?  I would next try checking for a sticking EGR valve.<BR>Speady<p>[This message has been edited by Speadraser (edited 09-21-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||WGraffiKs||09-21-1999||04:50 PM||||Occasionally, after sitting a while (cold) or warm, the engine in my '86 GT lets out a big puff of blue smoke when starting.  It starts within 1 second cold, 1-2 seconds when warm. Could this be the injectors leaking as well, or oil problems?  It doesn't seem to use much oil for 108,400 miles. The only oil leaks are the filler cap. Engine runs fine! ||||reg||5
Z||000014||GREENDANO||09-21-1999||05:46 PM||||Stupid question....Exactly how did you set the timing?  I set mine what was supposed to be the correct way and my brand new 3.2L did the same thing.  I also had a fuel pressure regulator failure too.  Fixed the reg. and it started beter, set the timing and it started perfectly, less than one revolution and it's running.  You did ground the ALDL pins A to B, right?<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||5
Z||000015||sluppy123||09-21-1999||07:08 PM||||Yes I ground out A and B on the ALDL conector||||reg||1
Z||000016||mwbackus||09-21-1999||11:46 PM||||WG :  That is quite typical of the V6 when there is oil leakage past a valve seal, or guide.  The seals can be replaced without head removal, and although it isnt what I consider an easy job, the seals are pretty cheap.  Valve guides DO require head removal to replace. You may want to do a compression check to better determine the condition of your heads, since valve train leakage can effect performance in a real bad way. :-(<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000017||Racer 1||09-22-1999||12:34 AM||||Cold start valve stuck open? I dont have 6 so I really dont know JACK about them.||||reg||14
Z||000018||Fab4mula||09-22-1999||12:40 PM||||Interesting.  This is what happens:<P>If the car is cold (sitting for quite a few hours), it kicks over immediately. <P>If I have driven the car, it cranks for a LONG time if I just turn the key.  Once it finally turns over, I get a small cloud of white smoke.<P>If I have driven the car, and I pt my foot to the floor and crank it, it turns over sooner than if I just crank it.  <P>All that and the car runs rich too.  I've replaced all of the ignition components, so I know they are good.  I've also replaced the temp sensors, the MAP sensor, the TPS sensor, O2 sensor, fuel filter, air filter, PCV valve, MAT sensor, IAC valve, and battery.  Based on all of this, I gotta believe there is at least one injector bad.  Must leak into the clinder and flood the motor when warm.  When the car gets cold and sits for a while, it's no longer flooded, so it starts fine.  Only when it takes forever to start does it smoke.  Never smokes any other time.  I'm thinking the white smoke is possibly moisture in fuel, or something along those lines.  It's not consuming any coolant.  Interesting.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Racer 1||09-22-1999||12:59 PM||||Fab4mula<P>I think you have a leaking or blown head gasket which is letting coolant into your cylinder. I had the same problem in my fiero I put that stop leak into the coolant and the white smoke went away!run you car then park it take out the spark plugs stick small white rope down in each cyld and see if it gets wet with antifreeze!!!!!||||reg||10
Z||000020||mwbackus||09-22-1999||02:03 PM||||I dunno.. usually, if the head gasket is bad enough to drench a cylinder it will overheat pretty bad.  The white smoke is pretty strange.  If it was condensation, you should still be able to check for a rich condition by placing your hand about an inch from the exhaust tip immediatly after start up.  If its rich it will coat your hand with carbon.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000021||Fab4mula||09-22-1999||02:49 PM||||It's weird.  I can smell that it's running rich, not to mention that the gas mileage is lousy.  It's not consuming ANY coolant, and oil usage is almost nil.  I DO have a leak near the main crank pulley, but that's all the oil I'm losing.  It's only a couple of drops a week.  I only get the smoke when it takes forever to start.  And I only get just a little bit.  Just a puff of smoke out of the pipes, and it stops.  I thought that it was oil, but the car isn't burning any when the engine is running.  I thought that it may be bad valve seals, letting oil drip into the cylinders, but again, it only smokes a bit and it only does it when I start the car when it is warm.  It's just plain posessed.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
